-component, the properties of integrals of vector functions are immediately inherited from those of the integrals of real-valued functions. \n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector-valued functions that are continuous on a closed interval $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha$ is a scalar. \nThen, the *vector addition and scalar multiplication rules* are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}(f(t)+g(t))\\text{d}t={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,+\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\, & \\text{vector addition rule} \\\\ {\\int }_{a}^{b}\\alpha \\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\alpha \\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\, & \\text{scalar constant multiple rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor example, if $\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: \\cos t\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf g(t) = \\left\\langle -t^2, \\: \\sin t \\right\\rangle,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int [f(t)+2g(t)]\\text{d}t & =\\int f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+2\\int g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t+2\\int ⟨-{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩+⟨2\\int -{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩+⟨-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{3},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3{t}^{2}-4{t}^{3}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $C_1, \\, C_2, \\, C_3, \\, C_4$ are arbitrary real constants and ${\\mathbf C} = \\langle C_1+C_3, C_2+C_4 \\rangle$ is a constant vector." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120995 #edb/ref 1065 2342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120995 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b3d07bd2-8d66-5645-a140-c638f5e9f002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120995 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121079 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ that is continuous on a closed interval $[a,b],$ the *rule for reversal of integration limits* states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{b}^{a}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121079 #edb/ref 1065 5177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121079 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "43078f08-37bf-59a3-8462-3b3efc865eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121079 #edb/ref 1067 "The Rule for Reversal of Integration Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121080 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ that is continuous on a closed interval $[a,b]$ and $p \\in (a,b),$ the *adjacent intervals rule* states the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{a}^{p}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{p}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121080 #edb/ref 1065 5178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121080 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ef0617f3-7ee0-5300-8795-10adbdc19d3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121080 #edb/ref 1067 "The Adjacent Intervals Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121081 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ is a vector-valued function that is continuous on a closed interval $[a,b]$ and $\\mathbf c$ is a constant vector. \nThen, the *products rules* are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}[c\\cdot f(t)]\\text{d}t=c\\cdot ({\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t)\\, & \\text{dot product rule} \\\\ {\\int }_{a}^{b}[c\\times f(t)]\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\, & \\text{cross product rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121081 #edb/ref 1065 5179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121081 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2531e67a-b5ed-5052-a97f-34abca386c58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121081 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rules" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300023 #edb/ref 1080 "2-e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300023 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300023 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f98e94d-1058-57cf-8d27-f13954103a7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300204 #edb/ref 1080 "3-2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300204 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300204 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "316106c6-12a5-5e17-af69-75de97025a36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300877 #edb/ref 1080 "3-e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300877 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300877 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b73bae5f-1557-57a4-b715-8b64982c76c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301146 #edb/ref 1080 "2+e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301146 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301146 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eef1692d-2f31-59e3-870d-7b705a603ab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301216 #edb/ref 1080 "3+e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301216 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301216 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fdc4d73d-2ea8-5c78-a393-c5faf89cb3ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186301146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186301216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302025 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa59178e-27aa-5b8c-b124-8431df83afc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302176 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186302025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302176 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302176 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302176 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})\\,i+{e}^{2}\\,j+(2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2})\\,k$ \nand \n$c={e}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2}\\,j-{e}^{2}\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302176 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "abace35f-02da-57bf-9b60-2e1f0caa0d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302176 #edb/ref 1074 "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({e}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2}\\,j-{e}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})\\,i+{e}^{2}\\,j+(2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2})\\,k) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{-1}-{e}^{-2})+{e}^{-2}\\cdot {e}^{2}-{e}^{2}\\cdot (2{e}^{-1}-3{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & =e-1+1-2e+3 \\\\  & =3-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358358 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨10,\\,6,\\,1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358358 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358358 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "46bf7675-80a2-559b-8162-709a1a0d7f2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358461 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨7,\\,2,\\,11⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358461 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358461 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52b55dd5-256e-5209-86de-a9c9700d2990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359017 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-10,\\,9,\\,-11⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359017 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359017 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "90b044f6-737d-5f41-adc8-0bdca392f181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359794 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨9,\\,1,\\,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359794 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359794 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6dcc4aa-84e5-52fb-b4ea-896d476393a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17